    <!-- Beacon my position over LoRa — opt-in, off by default, persisted off.
         The footer names what the switch EXPOSES, not only what it does: the frame
         leaves in cleartext to the broadcast address on the default LongFast channel,
         whose key is published in Meshtastic's own source. Airtime rules (15 min, 100 m,
         1 h ceiling) live in LoRaPositionBeacon and are repeated here in words. -->
    <string name="lora_position_section">Share my position</string>
    <string name="lora_position_toggle">Beacon my position over LoRa</string>
    <string name="lora_position_footer">Off by default. Every radio in range can read your position — up to tens of kilometres away, including people you have never met — and it is sent unencrypted, like Meshtastic does. Sent about every 15 minutes while OSHI is open, and only once you have moved.</string>

    <!-- Media over LoRa — opt-in, last-resort fallback with no internet at all. -->
    <string name="lora_media_section">Media over LoRa</string>
    <string name="lora_media_toggle">Send media over LoRa</string>
    <string name="lora_media_footer">Off by default. With no internet at all, this sends photos and documents very slowly over LoRa, in small pieces — expect several minutes, and only small images will fit.</string>

    <!-- Pre-send confirmation, shown only when a media send is about to ride LoRa
         (opted in, radio connected, no internet at all). Mirrors iOS keys
         "lora.media.confirm.*". %1$d is the estimated airtime in seconds; because
         anything that fits caps at 12 frames × 2 s, the estimate is always seconds
         here, so there is no minutes variant. -->
    <string name="lora_media_confirm_title">Send over LoRa?</string>
    <string name="lora_media_confirm_send">Send over LoRa</string>
    <string name="lora_media_confirm_body">There is no internet right now. This will send over LoRa in about %1$d seconds — slowly, and only small media fits. Send it?</string>
    <string name="lora_media_confirm_toobig">This looks too large for LoRa, where only small images fit. It will likely be refused. Try anyway?</string>

    <!-- ===================================================================
         NEW — interop reply to a bare/stock Meshtastic node (a "lora!<nodehex>"
         pseudo-identity thread). Short on purpose: it sits on every inbound
         bubble, on the header subtitle, and above the compose box, so it has
         to read at a glance, not as a paragraph. Said plainly rather than
         with the 🔒 this app uses for a REAL end-to-end thread, because this
         is the opposite of that: an unsigned node number on a public channel,
         not a key either side has proved. -->
    <string name="lora_interop_unverified">Unverified · plaintext</string>
    <string name="lora_interop_unverified_detail">This is a bare Meshtastic node, not an OSHI contact. Messages here are not end-to-end encrypted and the sender is not verified.</string>

    <!-- __NEVER_OVER_THE_INTERNET_2026_10_20__ Both rows say what they COST, not
         just what they do. "Prefer my radio" without "photos still use the
         network" reads as a privacy switch and behaves like a speed limit;
         "never send over the internet" without "incoming messages still arrive
         that way" claims more than the code delivers. -->
    <!-- __PIN_WITHOUT_A_SCREEN_2026_10_21__ The answer lived only on a forum.
         The "first boot" clause is what explains the failures: the pairing mode
         is fixed by whether a screen was present the first time the board
         booted, and removing it later does not change it. -->
    <string name="lora_pairing_noscreen_hint">No screen on your node? When it asks for a code, try 123456 — that is the default on boards that had no display the first time they booted.</string>

